        <!-- How the count works -->
        <div class="mt-12 p-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">How the Calculator Counts Years, Months and Days</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Age on official forms is counted in completed calendar units, not in blocks of 365 days, and that is what this page does. It subtracts the years first, then the months, then the days. When the day of the as-on date is smaller than your birth day, it borrows one month and adds the number of days in the month before the as-on date. When the month comes out negative, it borrows one year and adds twelve months. Someone born on 15 August 2000 is therefore 26 years, 1 month and 9 days old on 24 September 2026.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                The totals below the main result come from the exact number of days between the two dates. Total weeks is that figure divided by seven, total months is completed years times twelve plus completed months, and hours and minutes are the day count multiplied out. Only the calendar dates are used, so the time of day and your time zone never change the answer.
            </p>
            <h3 class="qt-h3 mb-3">Born on 29 February?</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
                A leap-day birthday exists only once every four years. In other years this calculator treats the year as complete on 1 March, the first day after 28 February, which is the convention most organisations follow, and the next-birthday countdown points to 1 March in those years. The total-days figure is unaffected, so nobody loses a day. A few bodies treat 28 February as the birthday instead; if a deadline hinges on it, check the rule with the office concerned.
            </p>
            <h3 class="qt-h3 mb-3">Age difference between two people</h3>
            <p>
                Enter the older person's date of birth as the Date of Birth and the younger person's date of birth in the As On field. The result is the exact gap between them in years, months and days, which is handy for sibling gaps, school admission pairs and insurance proposals that ask for a spouse's age.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Age as on -->
        <div class="mt-8 p-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Age As On a Cut-off Date: Exam, Job and Admission Forms</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Government exams, recruitment notices and school admissions never ask how old you are today. Each notification fixes one cut-off date and measures every applicant's age on that date, so two people applying on the same day can fall on opposite sides of the limit. The date is different for every exam and can change from year to year, so read it from the notification itself rather than from last year's form. The Civil Services Examination, for example, counts age as on 1 August of the exam year, while voter registration uses four qualifying dates a year (1 January, 1 April, 1 July and 1 October).
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                To check a limit, put the cut-off date in the As On field and read the completed years. A rule written as "must have attained 21 years and must not have attained 32 years as on 1 August 2026" is satisfied by anyone whose completed age on that date is 21 through 31. Most notifications also translate the limits into a window of birth dates ("born not earlier than 2 August 1994 and not later than 1 August 2005"), and checking your birth date against that window is the surest method. Age relaxations for reserved categories, ex-servicemen and persons with disabilities are added to the upper limit, and the notification lists them.
            </p>
            <p>
                Insurance works the other way round. Some insurers rate a policy on age nearest birthday instead of completed years, so a person aged 34 years and 7 months may be charged the premium for 35. The proposal form says which convention applies.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Milestones -->
        <div class="mt-8 p-8">
