                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">16</td><td>A learner's licence for a low-powered gearless two-wheeler is possible with a guardian's consent.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">18</td><td>Age of majority, the right to vote, a full driving licence, the capacity to sign contracts, a 10-year adult passport, and the legal marriage age for women.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">21</td><td>Legal marriage age for men under the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006, and the drinking age in several states.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">18 to 25</td><td>The drinking age is a state subject and varies within this range; some states prohibit alcohol entirely.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">60</td><td>Senior citizen for income tax (higher basic exemption under the old regime) and the retirement age for central government employees.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="font-semibold">80</td><td>Super senior citizen for income tax purposes.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Upper limits matter as much as lower ones. Recruitment notices, term insurance, home loans and visa categories all carry maximum ages, and each is measured on its own cut-off date.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Proof of DOB -->
        <div class="mt-8 p-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Documents That Prove Date of Birth</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li><strong>Birth certificate.</strong> Issued by the municipal or panchayat registrar under the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969. Since the 2023 amendment took effect on 1 October 2023, it is the single accepted proof of date of birth for anyone born on or after that date, for school admission, driving licences, voter registration, passports and government jobs.</li>
                <li><strong>Class 10 board certificate or marksheet.</strong> The document most authorities ask older applicants for, because the date of birth printed by the board is treated as the school record.</li>
                <li><strong>Passport.</strong> Accepted as proof of date of birth almost everywhere, and a minor's passport is valid for five years or until age 18.</li>
                <li><strong>PAN card.</strong> Carries the date of birth and is accepted by banks and many employers.</li>
                <li><strong>Aadhaar.</strong> Accepted for identity and address, but UIDAI treats it as proof of identity rather than of date of birth, and some authorities stopped accepting it on its own for that purpose in 2024.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Keep the date of birth identical across every document. A mismatch between a school certificate and a birth certificate is a common reason for passport and pension applications being held up, and the correction has to be made at the registrar or the board rather than at the office that spotted it.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Date formats -->
