            <p class="mb-4">
                India writes dates as day, month, year (05/08/2000 is 5 August 2000). The United States writes month first, so the same string means 8 May on an American website, and a date like 05/08 is ambiguous the moment it leaves the country. Spreadsheets and databases mostly use the international order year, month, day (2000-08-05), which is also what the date fields on this page store. A few habits avoid the mix-up:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
                <li>Write the month as a word wherever a form allows it: 5 August 2000.</li>
                <li>On a foreign website, check the placeholder text or a sample date before you type.</li>
                <li>Do not type a two-digit year. 05/08/00 can be read as 1900, 2000 or 2100 by different systems.</li>
                <li>Use the calendar picker on this page rather than typing, so the browser stores the date unambiguously.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

